>> title: Belly Lox
>> category: Cured Fish
>> tags: salmon, salt-cured, no-cook, appetizing, deli
>> counters: Deli
>> aka: lox, salt lox, salt-cured lox, belly lox, cured salmon, old-fashioned lox
>> pairs-with: bagels, cream-cheese, scallion-schmear
>> servings: 10
>> time: 3 days 14 hr
>> slack: unforgiving — three days in salt is what makes raw salmon safe to eat; cut the cure short and you have raw fish that only looks cured
>> step.1: pack the salmon in salt
>> step.2: cure 3 days, pour off the brine
>> step.3: rinse, soak 2 hr
>> step.4: pat dry, leave uncovered 12 hr
>> step.5: slice paper-thin off the skin

Pack @salmon fillet{2%lb}(900 g; belly end of a side, skin on, pin bones pulled) in @kosher salt{500%g}(about 2 1/2 cups) in a non-reactive dish, salt underneath and salt heaped over, so no flesh is showing.

Cover @&(~1)salted salmon{} and ~cure{3%days} in the fridge, pouring off the brine that collects each day and heaping the salt back over.

Rinse @&(~1)cured salmon{} under cold running water until no loose salt is left, then ~soak{2%hr} in @cold water{2%qt}(changed once).

Pat @&(~1)soaked salmon{} dry and ~dry{12%hr} uncovered on a rack in the fridge, until the surface is tacky rather than wet.

Slice @&(~1)dried lox{} on a long shallow angle down to the skin, as thin as your knife allows, and lay the slices flat. Serve with @lemon{1}(in wedges), @red onion{1/2}(sliced paper-thin) and @capers{1%Tbs}.
